WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:09.340
The movies that we make, Van [Neistat] and
I are in way communication aspects of the

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:14.890
sculpture.&nbsp; They are built like…the movies
are built, just like sculptures.&nbsp; They’re

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:23.670
films so they show the flaws, they are shot
with a Canon T2i with a missing pixel in the

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:28.200
middle about two-thirds of the way down from
the top of the screen you will see one red

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:34.640
dot.&nbsp; It’s almost always there and I think
we’re about to get a new version, like the

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:44.100
T8i or something?&nbsp; I’m not sure.&nbsp; But
it’s the idea that we started making movies

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:53.670
when iMovie came out and then iMovie started
to become so difficult and not functional.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:58.030
That is when we stopped for about three years
and now we’re using Final Cut Pro because

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.140
it’s easy again. Film Courage:&nbsp; What lenses
did you use because you had such great, intricate

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:08.319
close-up [shots] of drills and things like
that? Tom Sachs:&nbsp; That’s the off-the-shelf

00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:12.959
Canon zoom lens.&nbsp; I don’t even remember
the model number of it but it’s probably

00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:22.469
something like a 20 or 30 millimeter to zoom
out to be somewhere between 80 to 125.&nbsp; It’s

00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:26.360
a good, basic, off-the-shelf one. &nbsp; It’s
not even the one with the red ring on it.&nbsp;

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:32.110
It’s not the good one.&nbsp; I think we bought
one of those later when we had to get another

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:38.329
T2i to take a picture of our T2i for the credit
sequence in the SPACE PROGRAM.&nbsp; We needed

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:43.420
a photo of the camera so we had to buy a second
one just to take up one photo of the first

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.649
one…which we then returned.
Film Courage:&nbsp; So the entire film is shot

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:52.909
on a Canon T2i.&nbsp; Wow!… Tom Sachs:&nbsp; It’s
name is Baby Jesus and in the credit sequence

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:57.700
you’ll see that…in the credit sequence
shot…you’ll see the camera is in it.&nbsp;

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:07.569
There’s some iPhone footage also, but nothing…there
is nothing exotic.&nbsp; When we shoot still footage

00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:15.220
of the sculptures those are all shot with
like a…40 ninja pixel Hasselblad digital

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:21.049
back.&nbsp; Those are always shot by Genevieve
Hanson or Joshua White, those are stills and

00:02:21.049 --> 00:02:24.469
those are for images you’ll see in books
and on websites. Film Courage:&nbsp; What was

00:02:24.469 --> 00:02:29.200
your decision to use the T2i?&nbsp; I mean I was
thinking you used a Red camera or something.&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:35.280
It’s beautiful… Tom Sachs:&nbsp; It’s really
what Van had…that’s Van’s camera and

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:45.709
what’s important about the T2i to our team
is that Van knows what all the buttons do.&nbsp;

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:52.150
And the Red has…it’s probably a superior
camera in nearly every way except you have

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:59.379
a huge liability because it’s so expensive
and the virtue of a cheap camera is that it’s

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:04.780
not an heirloom product.&nbsp; It’s a consumable
even though a camera is not typically understood

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.581
as a consumable.&nbsp; The films, the data cards,
are considered consumables.&nbsp; But when something

00:03:08.581 --> 00:03:15.829
is that cheap you can take risks.&nbsp; You don’t
have to obsess about it and I think a lot

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:21.659
of filmmakers and camera aficionados are more
obsessed with the hardware than what it’s

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:26.599
capturing and if something is only $500 and
you’ve got a huge production ahead of you,

00:03:26.599 --> 00:03:33.530
it’s really a relatively small part and
that reversal of economics and power is what

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:38.159
gives us the freedom to make a movie like
we did.&nbsp; It’s a a bar-lowering affect.&nbsp;

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:45.640
And, I can’t underscore this enough, the
knowing how to use the tool is the most important

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:52.099
thing.&nbsp; Not what the tool can do.&nbsp; As a
sculptor I made much better work with lower

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:59.959
quality tools that were in my hands that I
know I have a muscle memory with a more precise,

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:04.270
better tool because it’s part of my body.&nbsp;
I’m not thinking. So when Van is getting

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.760
these shots, he doesn’t have to worry about
learning about some great new feature.&nbsp; He

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:08.760
just goes…QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS: What’s
the cheapest camera and most expensive camera

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:08.770
you have worked with? How would you compare
the experiences?

